{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## THE READ SIDE IS UP — Gerrit 1324. Stacked on 1319/1320, above 1311.\n\n`get_parent_map` now reads a non-sub-task's parent from the \"Epic Link\" field as well as\n`fields.parent`, closing the round trip 1311 left open.\n\n**Discovery is SHARED, not duplicated** — the point of the ticket. 1311's lookup was inline inside\n`set_parent`; it is now extracted to `_resolve_epic_link_field_id()` and BOTH `set_parent` and\n`get_parent_map` call it. One discovery path, one cache, so the write side and the read side cannot\ndisagree about which field they mean. A second parallel lookup would have been the obvious shortcut\nand the wrong one.\n\n**A real regression was caught during the extraction, and it is worth recording.** The inline code\nread `self._epic_link_field_id` as a bare attribute, which breaks for a transport built via\n`__new__` — several existing pagination tests do exactly that, skipping `__init__`. Wiring\n`get_parent_map` straight to the attribute turned 3 of them RED. Fixed with `getattr`/`setattr`\nagainst the `_MISSING` default: same caching, tolerant of a skipped `__init__`. **1311 shipped this\nlatent** — it only ever ran through `set_parent`, whose callers all construct normally, so nothing\nexercised it. Extending a cached attribute to a second caller is what exposed it.\n\n**RED evidence, captured against the REBASED baseline** (`b301e7513d`) by stashing the change and\nre-running — not against a stale base:\n * `test_epic_parent_is_read_back_from_the_epic_link_field` → `{'DC-10': None}`, expected `DC-1`\n * `test_field_discovery_failure_hits_the_same_degradation_contract` → `{'DC-14': 'DC-5'}`, expected `{}`\n * `test_discovery_is_shared_with_set_parent_not_duplicated` → `{'DC-15': None}`, expected `DC-1`\n\nReported honestly by the implementer: **4 of the 7 cells passed trivially pre-fix** — they assert\nbehaviour that was already incidentally true. Those four are weak and should not be counted as\nevidence; the three above are what discriminate.\n\nGates: ruff clean, mypy 421, hygiene 0, **2105 unit tests pass**, module-size contract passes.\n\n### FLAGGED — `transport.py` is at 799/800\n\nOne line of headroom. This is now the THIRD time this file has forced prose-trimming to fit\n(780 → 794 → 781 → 799 across 1302, 1305, 1311 and this change), and the next parent-related change\ncannot land without a split. Per this epic's own precedent — b586 hit the same wall and was split\ninto a mechanical relocation (S1) followed by the policy change (S2), and S1 passed plan review\nFIRST TRY where the combined ticket had taken ten rounds — the split should be its own change,\nBEFORE more work lands here. Not filed unilaterally; recorded for the operator, since it is a\nrestructuring of a file three in-flight changes touch.\n\n### STILL NOT PROVEN\n\nThat the read actually works against DC 8.17.1 — AC3 requires a harness run, and AC4's idempotence\nquestion (does a converged epic-parent pair stop re-emitting?) is exactly the churn risk this\nticket was filed with. Neither is settled by 2105 green unit tests; 1276, 1288 and 1302 all had\nthat and were all wrong live."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785796708690581001,"uuid":"316e7edb-c7b4-434c-ae9a-3d31a6b72da1"}